Research Logbook Entry
Hours Worked: 7 hours
Project: MW–M31 Halo Shape Evolution and Dynamical Visualization
Date: March 5, 2026


--------------------------------------------------------------------
Objectives for the Day
--------------------------------------------------------------------

The primary objective for today was to continue expanding the
visualization framework for the MW–M31 merger analysis pipeline.
The focus was on building additional movie-generation modules
that illustrate dynamical quantities derived from the simulation
snapshots. These movies complement earlier plots and analysis by
allowing time-resolved visual inspection of the physical processes
occurring during the merger.

A secondary goal was to ensure that the visualization modules are
efficient, modular, and consistent with the rest of the research
pipeline.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Work Completed
--------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Continued Development of Movie Generation Framework

Additional movie modules were designed and implemented using
Matplotlib's FFMpegWriter interface. These modules extend the
existing visualization pipeline and allow different dynamical
quantities to be tracked over time across all simulation snapshots.

Each movie focuses on a specific physical property in order to
avoid visual overcrowding and to isolate the physical processes
being examined.

2. Implementation of Angular Momentum Vector Evolution Movie

A new module was implemented to visualize the evolution of the
specific angular momentum vector of the combined MW–M31 halo.

The movie plots the trajectory of the angular momentum vector
(j_x, j_y, j_z) in three-dimensional space across all snapshots.

This visualization is intended to reveal:

• orbital angular momentum transfer  
• merger-driven torques  
• spin reorientation or spin flips  
• final angular momentum stabilization after halo coalescence  

The algorithm iteratively plots the trajectory of the vector
through time, updating the current position at each frame.

The computational cost of this visualization is low because the
movie uses precomputed angular momentum vectors rather than
recalculating particle-level quantities.

3. Implementation of Spin Parameter Evolution Movie

A second movie module was implemented to visualize the evolution
of the Bullock spin parameter (λ') across the merger timeline.

This parameter measures the rotational support of the halo
relative to its gravitational binding energy.

The movie progressively plots λ' as a function of snapshot
number, allowing the temporal evolution of halo spin to be
observed.

This visualization is designed to highlight:

• spin fluctuations during close passages  
• angular momentum injection during the merger  
• relaxation toward a stable post-merger halo configuration  

As with the angular momentum visualization, the movie uses
precomputed time-series data, keeping runtime and memory
requirements minimal.

4. Detailed Commentary and Computational Analysis

Extensive commentary was added to the code explaining:

• physical meaning of the quantities being visualized  
• algorithmic workflow of each module  
• time complexity of the frame-generation process  
• memory usage and scalability considerations  

This documentation ensures that the visualization modules are
transparent and reproducible, which is critical for research
projects intended for publication or presentation.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Technical Notes
--------------------------------------------------------------------

The movie-generation modules operate on time-series arrays
generated earlier in the analysis pipeline.

Because the expensive particle-level computations are already
completed prior to visualization, the movie generation stage
is computationally lightweight.

Typical runtime scaling:

Frame generation complexity: O(N_snapshots²)

However, with approximately 800 snapshots this remains
computationally trivial.

Memory requirements are also minimal because only small
time-series arrays are used rather than full particle data.

